{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789231271,"nonce":"Vo3rbunibJFDgToxPVfKGHvFbkM=","title":"an element yielded from a Dynamic array is raw: 0 prints as null, a String element as \u003cunknown type\u003e","message":"var t = new haxe.Template(\"::foreach bar:: ::foo:: ::__current__:: ::end::\");\n    t.execute({foo: '!', bar: [0, 1]});            // \" ! null  ! 1 \"  (Issue9372)\n    new haxe.Template(\"::foreach names:: hello ::__current__:: ::end::\")\n        .execute({names: [\"wwx2016\"]});            // \" hello \u003cunknown type N\u003e \" (Issue5293)\n\n`for (x in it)` over a Dynamic iterator (7f48c1ae) yields each element as\nthe raw i64 the array stores, under a Dynamic type. A HaxeArray records no\nelement kind, so the handle cannot box the element as what it is: a raw 0\nreads as null, a raw HaxeString pointer reads as a box with an unknown\ntag. The typed for-in never sees this because the element type is static.\n\nFix direction: carry an element tag on HaxeArray (set where the array is\ncreated, every creation site knows its element type) and box on yield from\nthe Dynamic handle; or box elements on store into an Array\u003cDynamic\u003e, which\nbugs_dynamic_array_elements_stored_raw already measured as a whole-program\nchange.","files":null}]}